science/article/pii/S2667325826004255)\n  57. NASA probe brings back samples from asteroid Bennu, discovers diverse amino acids: International research group confirmed the discovery of \"Materials responsible for life\" (https://sj.jst.go.jp/news/202503/n0324-01k.html)\n  58. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://pubs.acs.org/doi/abs/10.1021/cg901303a)\n  59. Thermal decomposition of the amino acids glycine, cysteine, aspartic acid, asparagine, glutamic acid, glutamine, arginine and histidine - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5807855/)\n  60. Phase Transformations of Glutamic Acid and Its Decomposition Products (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/231233025_Phase_Transformations_of_Glutamic_Acid_and_Its_Decomposition_Products)\n  61. The effects of temperature, pH and redox state on the stability of glutamic acid in hydrothermal fluids - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0016703714001537)\n  62. Enantiomeric Excesses of Acid Labile Amino Acid Precursors of the Murchison Meteorite - NASA Technical Reports Server (NTRS) (https://ntrs.nasa.gov/search.jsp?R=19990021044&hterms=pyroglutamic+acid&qs=N%3D0%26Ntk%3DAll%26Ntx%3Dmode%2Bmatchall%26Ntt%3Dpyroglutamic%2Bacid)\n\n=== END DEEP RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\n\n\n=== END LITERATURE RESEARCH FINDINGS ===\n\nINSTRUCTIONS FOR LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS:\n1. Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the research above.\n2. Weigh the critiques and contested points the research reports; do not adopt a route uncritically.\n3. You may depart from the described routes when the chemistry justifies it; explain why in \"reasoning\".\n4. Cite the supporting paper (author, year) in each reaction's \"reasoning\" field, or mark the reaction as speculative.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.46\n- Feedback: Topology is adequate: 14 molecule nodes and 12 reaction nodes form a connected network with consistent IDs, and the dual-module strategy matches the two major glutamate families in the literature. The blocking issue is validity: the RDKit balance check flags 11 of 12 reactions as unbalanced, so the network is not mass-conserving as written; only rxn_009 is balanced. rxn_001 and rxn_002 need explicit coproduct or intermediate bookkeeping from the alpha-ketoglutarate-forming sequences, rxn_003 and rxn_004 need explicit reductant/electron bookkeeping rather than direct alpha-ketoglutarate + NH3 -> glutamate, rxn_005, rxn_006, rxn_010, and rxn_012 likely need H2O products, rxn_007 and rxn_011 cannot be repaired by a single small species and should include hydrolysis water plus NH3/CO2-type byproducts, and rxn_008 should be split into the Patel maleonitrile -> succinonitrile -> 3-cyanopropanal sequence with UV/HS-/Cu and an explicit oxygen source. Environmental consistency is mixed because 'Biochemical' is not a physical prebiotic environment, the cyanosulfidic branch lacks UV/Cu/sequential-delivery detail, and transfers between hydrothermal and surface modules are not justified. Literature alignment is otherwise good: the network explicitly includes both alpha-KG and nitrile families, treats the acrolein branch as speculative, and includes pyroglutamate as a sink/reservoir, but it does not solve the literature-highlighted one-pot coupling problem and rxn_012 should also release H2O if retained. Overall, this is a strategically well-informed but currently nonviable network specification, and no pathway reaches approval.\n- Issues: [\"Conceptually coherent and supported in pieces by the literature, but not valid as written. rxn_001 is unbalanced and appears to omit an oxygen-containing coproduct or intermediate bookkeeping from the glyoxylate + pyruvate sequence. rxn_003 is more serious: direct alpha-ketoglutarate + NH3 -> glutamate is unbalanced, and as written the atom count would only close via an implausible O2 release; the fresh-FeS redox partner and water/byproducts are missing. The Hydrothermal -> Biochemical handoff is also not physically justified, and 'Biochemical' is not a prebiotic environment label.\", 'This is the best-aligned hydrothermal branch because both modules map onto strong literature precedents and stay in a vent-like setting. It still fails conservation: rxn_001 is unbalanced, and rxn_004 omits the electrochemical/redox bookkeeping needed for electroreduced FeS amination; as written, direct alpha-ketoglutarate + NH3 -> glutamate leaves extra oxygen unaccounted for. The pathway idea is strong, but the network specification is not chemically valid until the reduced mineral state, electron source, and coproducts are explicit.', \"The separate chemistry is plausible and literature-backed: Fe2+ chemistry can access alpha-ketoglutarate and Ni/H2 can aminate alpha-ketoglutarate. As written, rxn_001 remains unbalanced, and rxn_005 is also unbalanced and is most naturally corrected by adding H2O as product. The Ni/H2 step is mislabeled 'Biochemical'; the cited chemistry is mineral-catalyzed aqueous reduction and should also acknowledge the competing alpha-hydroxyglutarate channel reported under Ni/H2 conditions.\", 'This Bucherer-Bergs-type bridge is literature-aligned in spirit, but the node definitions are too compressed. rxn_006 is unbalanced and likely needs H2O as coproduct; it also cites DAP without including DAP as a reactant node. rxn_007 cannot be written as glutamate hydantoin -> glutamate alone; the balance check shows no single common small species fixes it, so hydrolysis water and carbon/nitrogen byproducts such as NH3 and CO2 must be explicit. The Hydrothermal -> Surface transfer is also left unexplained.', \"The phosphate alpha-ketoglutarate module and fresh FeS amination each have literature precedent, but the pathway is weak as specified. rxn_002 is unbalanced and needs explicit coproduct or intermediate bookkeeping from the phosphate-mediated alpha-ketoacid network. rxn_003 is unbalanced and lacks the reducing/mineral stoichiometry for FeS amination, with the present formula bookkeeping effectively implying an implausible O2 release. The jump from a phosphate-rich surface setting to a 'Biochemical' environment is unsupported.\", 'This branch combines two experimentally grounded modules, and the electroreduced FeS amination has strong literature support. However, rxn_002 remains unbalanced, and rxn_004 omits the redox bookkeeping required for electroreduced FeS chemistry. The surface-to-hydrothermal transfer is possible in principle but is not physically described, so environmental continuity is only moderate.', \"Chemically this is one of the stronger two-step ideas: phosphate chemistry can give alpha-ketoglutarate, and Ni/H2 chemistry can aminate it. But the network still violates conservation: rxn_002 is unbalanced, and rxn_005 is missing H2O as product according to the balance check. The Ni/H2 step is again mislabeled 'Biochemical' rather than a defined mineral-catalyzed aqueous setting, and the literature warns that alpha-hydroxyglutarate competes strongly under Ni/H2 conditions.\", 'Among the surface-only branches, this is the most environmentally coherent and it follows the literature bridge from alpha-ketoacids to hydantoins. The problems are stoichiometric and specification-level: rxn_002 is unbalanced, rxn_006 likely needs H2O as coproduct and should include DAP explicitly, and rxn_007 is not mass-conserving as written and should include hydrolysis water plus carbon/nitrogen byproducts. The pathway concept is sound, but the final closure to glutamate is not correctly encoded.', 'This captures the cyanosulfidic glutamate family only very approximately. rxn_008 is the main failure: the published chemistry is a multi-step UV/HS-/Cu sequence through maleonitrile and succinonitrile, whereas the network collapses it into an atom-nonconserving single step from cyanoacetylene + HCN + H2S to 3-cyanopropanal with no oxygen source. rxn_010 is close but missing H2O as product, and rxn_011 cannot be reduced to aminonitrile -> glutamate without explicit hydrolysis water and NH3 release. As written this pathway is not chemically valid.', \"The opening step is the network's only balanced reaction: rxn_009 is a chemically plausible HCN conjugate addition to acrolein. However, the literature treats this glutamate branch as speculative rather than experimentally closed. rxn_010 is unbalanced and likely needs H2O as product, while rxn_011 is severely underspecified because aminonitrile hydrolysis to glutamate must include water consumption and NH3 release. This pathway has a reasonable start but does not yet deliver a valid balanced route to glutamate.\"]\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.85\n- Feedback: Connected, modular, and strongly aligned with the literature review. The network reaches glutamate by several plausible branches and correctly keeps alpha-KG, 3-cyanopropanal, and pyroglutamate explicit as bottlenecks or reservoirs. Network validity is high: there are no isolated subgraphs and the RDKit check reports no unbalanced reactions, although rxn_007 should be written with 2 H2O and rxn_010 with 4 H2O and 2 NH3. The strongest chemistry is the alpha-KG module, especially the hydrothermal Fe2+ plus FeS closure and the alternative Ni/H2 closure, which is consistent with the separate experimental literature from Muchowska or Stubbs, Huber and Wachtershauser or Kitadai, and Kaur. The hydantoin bridge also aligns well with Pulletikurti in spirit, though its reagents and hydrolysis limitations are under-specified here. Main remaining weaknesses are that rxn_001 and rxn_002 are compressed net transformations, rxn_008 is only weakly supported in a prebiotic context, and rxn_012 overstates how easily pyroglutamate reopens. Overall this is a good, literature-aware network, but it is not approved at the network threshold because several key edges remain shorthand or contested rather than a fully closed, environment-specific end-to-end sequence.\n- Issues: ['Coherent alpha-KG branch, but glycine-to-glutamate transamination is less directly established for alpha-KG under prebiotic conditions than FeS or Ni/H2 amination. The hydrothermal-to-surface handoff is acceptable only if transport and concentration are made explicit.', 'The hydantoin bridge is literature-aligned and sensibly keeps cyanide chemistry explicit, but rxn_006 is under-specified relative to the reported alpha-KG hydantoin chemistry, and rxn_007 should be written as hydantoin plus 2 H2O giving glutamate plus NH3 plus CO2. Hydrolysis to glutamate is experimentally incomplete rather than a clean quantitative closure.', 'Conceptually coherent, but both steps are the less secure versions of the alpha-KG module. Phosphate-generated alpha-KG is supported, yet glycine transamination to glutamate needs clearer direct experimental anchoring and may require conditions not fully compatible with the phosphate stage.', 'All-surface hydantoin route fits the modular strategy, but it inherits the compressed phosphate alpha-KG step and the low-conversion hydantoin hydrolysis problem. rxn_007 needs an extra H2O coefficient, and the actual literature route benefits from more explicit reagent and temperature staging.', 'Strecker logic is correct and the network appropriately keeps 3-cyanopropanal explicit, but the weakest edge is still rxn_008: acrolein to 3-cyanopropanal is chemically plausible yet only proposed, not convincingly demonstrated under prebiotic conditions. rxn_010 must be corrected to aminonitrile plus 4 H2O giving glutamate plus 2 NH3.', 'Including pyroglutamate is a literature-aligned strength because glutamate readily cyclizes, but the reverse step is drawn too simply: pyroglutamate reopening is possible yet usually harsher or slower than generic surface hydration. This branch captures a realistic sink or reservoir, but not an easy reversible loop.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Glutamate\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"The network is centered on alpha-ketoglutarate because it is the best-supported glutamate-specific hub in the literature. A common aldol step from glyoxylate and pyruvate gives hydroxyketoglutarate, after which alpha-ketoglutarate is reached by either an Fe2+/H2-assisted net dehydration-reduction route or a phosphate/glyoxylate route that explicitly produces oxalate. From alpha-ketoglutarate, five alternative closures to glutamate are represented: fresh FeS reductive amination, electroreduced FeS reductive amination, Ni/H2 reductive amination, glycine transamination, and cyanide-to-hydantoin capture followed by hydrolysis. The main competing or reservoir channels highlighted by the literature are also explicit: alpha-hydroxyglutarate under hydrogenation conditions and pyroglutamate from glutamate cyclization. Direct 3-cyanopropanal Strecker routes are not expanded separately because their C4 precursor supply is less experimentally closed than the alpha-ketoacid family for glutamate.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxylate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopropanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyruvate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O6\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"4-hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydroxyketoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H9NO4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminopentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)\",\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H5NO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-aminoacetic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycine\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"HCN\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N2O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-(2-carboxyethyl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glutamate hydantoin\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"oxalate\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H2O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H7NO3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"5-oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"pyroglutamate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H8O5\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxypentanedioic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"alpha-hydroxyglutarate\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Aldol addition of glyoxylate and pyruvate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic concentrated geothermal pool; Fe2+ or phosphate may be present but are not required for the core aldol step.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Pyruvate enolization followed by aldol addition to glyoxylate gives the C5 beta-hydroxy alpha-keto diacid hydroxyketoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aldol union of pyruvate and glyoxylate is the documented entry step in both the Fe2+-promoted TCA-analogue network and the phosphate-only alpha-ketoacid network (Muchowska et al., 2019; Stubbs et al., 2020). Kaur et al. (2024) further showed that the aldol condensation can occur without added metal, so this is one of the least contested entries into glutamate chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+/H2 net dehydration-reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"340-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic Fe2+-rich hot spring or geothermal pool with dissolved H2; near-neutral pH.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydroxyketoglutarate undergoes net dehydration to an unsaturated oxo-diacid and subsequent reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Muchowska et al. (2019) showed Fe2+-promoted conversion of pyruvate and glyoxylate through hydroxyketoglutarate to alpha-ketoglutarate at about 70 C. The exact downstream redox bookkeeping was not isolated as a single elementary step; this node is therefore a literature-guided, mass-balanced net representation of dehydration plus reduction, with H2 inserted as the geochemically plausible reducing equivalent in warm anoxic Fe2+-rich waters.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate/glyoxylate conversion to alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"phosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral pH, concentrated phosphate-rich pool, excess glyoxylate, anoxic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"A second glyoxylate acts as reductant while hydroxyketoglutarate undergoes net dehydration-reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate; the sacrificial glyoxylate is oxidized to oxalate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stubbs et al. (2020) reported a metal-free, phosphate-catalyzed alpha-ketoacid network in which glyoxylate acts as both carbon source and reductant, producing alpha-ketoglutarate in about 5% yield at pH 7 and 50 C. Writing oxalate explicitly as the coproduct makes the redox closure transparent and avoids the under-specified shorthand often used for this step.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Fresh-FeS reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"fresh FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"323-348K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"fresh FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide-rich hot spring sediment or geothermal precipitate; pH near the NH4+/NH3 pKa; explicit H2 denotes net reducing equivalents.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate first forms an imine with ammonia, then a reduced FeS surface delivers electrons and protons to give glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Freshly precipitated FeS and related reduced iron minerals promote reductive amination of alpha-oxo acids near the pKa of ammonium, and glutamate is among the reported products (Hafenbradl et al., 1995; Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003). Because published glutamate yields are often summarized secondarily and alpha-ketoglutarate is intrinsically the slowest common ketoacid to aminate, this remains a bottleneck rather than a solved step (Mayer and Moran, 2022). The explicit H2 is a net redox bookkeeping device rather than a claim that free H2 was the only laboratory reductant.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Electroreduced-FeS reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"electroreduced FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"electroreduced FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic sulfide interface with natural redox disequilibrium; explicit H2 denotes reducing equivalents stored in the mineral surface.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia adds to the ketoacid and the resulting imine is reduced on an electron-rich FeS surface.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kitadai et al. (2019) showed that electrochemically reduced FeS converts alpha-keto acids to amino acids in high yield, with alanine and glutamate favored. The experimental setting was an alkaline-vent analogue; placing the same chemistry at a redox-active surface sulfide interface is an environmental extrapolation, but it retains the strongest direct support for preferential glutamate formation on FeS.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Ni/H2 reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\", \"Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Ambient to mildly elevated H2 fugacity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"pH around 8; NH3 or NH4HCO3-rich pool; competing alpha-hydroxyglutarate formation is expected.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate forms an imine with ammonia and heterogeneous Ni activates H2 to hydrogenate that intermediate to glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-keto acids over Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, Pd/C, and Ni-bearing meteorite material at 22 C and pH 8 with much lower ammonia than older Fe-based studies. This step is experimentally strong for isolated alpha-keto acids, but their one-pot alpha-ketoglutarate-forming network still did not detect glutamate, and hydroxy acids competed strongly with Ni and meteorite catalysts (Kaur et al., 2024).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycine transamination of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Near-neutral pH, concentrated aqueous pool, anoxic.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Amino transfer from glycine to the alpha-ketoacid converts alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate while oxidizing glycine to glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Stubbs et al. (2020) reported that alpha-ketoacids from the phosphate/glyoxylate network can be transaminated by glycine, which makes glutamate formation metabolically congruent and avoids mineral-mediated reductive amination. Direct prebiotic alpha-ketoglutarate-to-glutamate data are less complete than for FeS or Ni/H2, so this edge is included as a plausible but weaker closure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydantoin capture of alpha-ketoglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"diamidophosphate\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-305K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"diamidophosphate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Cyanide-bearing pool with staged reagent delivery; the node tracks the balanced organic core and does not expand phosphate-containing byproducts from DAP turnover.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Cyanide captures the keto carbonyl, ammonia incorporation follows, and DAP-assisted cyclization yields the 5-substituted hydantoin.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) converted alpha-ketoacids, including alpha-ketoglutarate, to hydantoins in very high yield using cyanide with diamidophosphate activation, explicitly proposing this as a bridge between cyanide chemistry and alpha-ketoacid metabolism. The route is experimentally strong for hydantoin formation, but reagent plausibility remains debated because it depends on cyanide and DAP.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of glutamate hydantoin\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_011\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-355K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Warm hydrolysis over prolonged times; incomplete conversion expected.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Hydantoin ring opening followed by hydrolytic decarbamylation releases glutamate, ammonia, and carbon dioxide.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pulletikurti et al. (2022) demonstrated hydrolytic conversion of the alpha-ketoglutarate hydantoin to glutamate, but the closure was inefficient: the reported preprint data gave about 19% glutamate with most hydantoin still present after hydrolysis. This is therefore a real but low-conversion end step rather than a clean quantitative closure.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Competitive reduction to alpha-hydroxyglutarate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3\", \"Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-310K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"Ambient to mildly elevated H2 fugacity\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Anoxic pH around 8; direct competitor to Ni-mediated amination.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Heterogeneous hydrogenation reduces the alpha-keto group directly to the corresponding alpha-hydroxy acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Kaur et al. (2024) observed amino acid and hydroxy acid mixtures from alpha-keto acid hydrogenations, with Ni and meteoritic catalysts often giving near 1:1 amino-to-hydroxy ratios. Including alpha-hydroxyglutarate explicitly captures the main competing sink that erodes glutamate yield; the general kinetic caution is also consistent with Mayer and Moran (2022).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Cyclization of glutamate to pyroglutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"333-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Accelerated by acidic wet-dry cycles and heating; stronger heating eventually causes further decomposition.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Intramolecular condensation of the amino group with the gamma-carboxyl group forms the 5-membered lactam and expels water.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glutamate cyclizes to pyroglutamate in warm or acidic aqueous settings and during drying; heating also drives the same dehydration in the solid state (Lee et al., 2014; Wu et al., 2010; Weiss et al., 2018). Because meteoritic work treats pyroglutamate as an acid-labile precursor reservoir for glutamate, this species should be represented as more than a simple dead-end loss channel (Pizzarello and Cooper, 2001).\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolytic reopening of pyroglutamate\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"Slow reservoir release under prolonged aqueous exposure or harsher hydrolysis.\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Lactam hydrolysis reopens the pyroglutamate ring to free glutamate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Pyroglutamate can reopen to glutamate under hydrolytic conditions, which is why meteoritic pyroglutamate is treated as an acid-labile glutamate reservoir (Pizzarello and Cooper, 2001). However, reopening is slower and generally harsher than cyclization, so this edge is included as a weak reservoir-release step rather than an efficient equilibrium (Lee et al., 2014).\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+/H2 feeder plus fresh-FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydroxyketoglutarate is formed by aldol chemistry, converted to alpha-ketoglutarate by the Fe2+/H2 net route, and then reductively aminated on fresh FeS.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+/H2 feeder plus electroreduced-FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The Fe2+/H2 feeder generates alpha-ketoglutarate, which is then aminated on an electron-rich FeS surface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+/H2 feeder plus Ni/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate from the Fe2+/H2 feeder is hydrogenatively aminated on Ni-bearing mineral or meteoritic surfaces.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+/H2 feeder plus glycine transamination\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate from the Fe2+/H2 feeder accepts nitrogen from glycine, regenerating glyoxylate and producing glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Fe2+/H2 feeder plus hydantoin bridge\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate from the Fe2+/H2 feeder is trapped as a glutamate hydantoin and then hydrolyzed to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus fresh-FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"The phosphate/glyoxylate route generates alpha-ketoglutarate with oxalate coproduct, then fresh FeS closes the network to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus electroreduced-FeS amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate made by the phosphate/glyoxylate route is aminated on an electron-rich FeS surface.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus Ni/H2 amination\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate formed in the phosphate-driven alpha-ketoacid network is converted to glutamate by Ni-catalyzed hydrogenative amination.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus glycine transamination\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate from the phosphate/glyoxylate feeder is transaminated by glycine to produce glutamate and recycle glyoxylate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus hydantoin bridge\",\n      \"description\": \"Alpha-ketoglutarate from the phosphate/glyoxylate feeder is captured by cyanide as a hydantoin and hydrolyzed to glutamate.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_008\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_011\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Glutamate", "overall_strategy": "The network is centered on alpha-ketoglutarate because it is the best-supported glutamate-specific hub in the literature. A common aldol step from glyoxylate and pyruvate gives hydroxyketoglutarate, after which alpha-ketoglutarate is reached by either an Fe2+/H2-assisted net dehydration-reduction route or a phosphate/glyoxylate route that explicitly produces oxalate. From alpha-ketoglutarate, five alternative closures to glutamate are represented: fresh FeS reductive amination, electroreduced FeS reductive amination, Ni/H2 reductive amination, glycine transamination, and cyanide-to-hydantoin capture followed by hydrolysis. The main competing or reservoir channels highlighted by the literature are also explicit: alpha-hydroxyglutarate under hydrogenation conditions and pyroglutamate from glutamate cyclization. Direct 3-cyanopropanal Strecker routes are not expanded separately because their C4 precursor supply is less experimentally closed than the alpha-ketoacid family for glutamate.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "C2H2O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxoacetic acid", "common_name": "glyoxylate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O3/c3-1-2(4)5/h1H,(H,4,5)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C3H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxopropanoic acid", "common_name": "pyruvate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H4O3/c1-2(4)3(5)6/h1H3,(H,5,6)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C5H6O6", "iupac_name": "4-hydroxy-2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "hydroxyketoglutarate", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C5H6O5", "iupac_name": "2-oxopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "alpha-ketoglutarate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H6O5/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h1-2H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "C5H9NO4", "iupac_name": "2-aminopentanedioic acid", "common_name": "glutamate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C5H9NO4/c6-3(5(9)10)1-2-4(7)8/h3H,1-2,6H2,(H,7,8)(H,9,10)", "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "C2H5NO2", "iupac_name": "2-aminoacetic acid", "common_name": "glycine", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H5NO2/c3-1-2(4)5/h1,3H2,(H,4,5)", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "common_name": "HCN", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "C6H8N2O4", "iupac_name": "5-(2-carboxyethyl)imidazolidine-2,4-dione", "common_name": "glutamate hydantoin", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C2H2O4", "iupac_name": "ethanedioic acid", "common_name": "oxalate", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H2O4/c3-1(4)2(5)6/h(H,3,4)(H,5,6)", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "CO2", "iupac_name": "carbon dioxide", "common_name": "carbon dioxide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CO2/c2-1-3", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C5H7NO3", "iupac_name": "5-oxopyrrolidine-2-carboxylic acid", "common_name": "pyroglutamate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_015", "formula": "C5H8O5", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxypentanedioic acid", "common_name": "alpha-hydroxyglutarate", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Aldol addition of glyoxylate and pyruvate", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_003"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic concentrated geothermal pool; Fe2+ or phosphate may be present but are not required for the core aldol step."}, "mechanism": "Pyruvate enolization followed by aldol addition to glyoxylate gives the C5 beta-hydroxy alpha-keto diacid hydroxyketoglutarate.", "reasoning": "Aldol union of pyruvate and glyoxylate is the documented entry step in both the Fe2+-promoted TCA-analogue network and the phosphate-only alpha-ketoacid network (Muchowska et al., 2019; Stubbs et al., 2020). Kaur et al. (2024) further showed that the aldol condensation can occur without added metal, so this is one of the least contested entries into glutamate chemistry."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Fe2+/H2 net dehydration-reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Fe2+"], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "Fe2+", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic Fe2+-rich hot spring or geothermal pool with dissolved H2; near-neutral pH."}, "mechanism": "Hydroxyketoglutarate undergoes net dehydration to an unsaturated oxo-diacid and subsequent reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate.", "reasoning": "Muchowska et al. (2019) showed Fe2+-promoted conversion of pyruvate and glyoxylate through hydroxyketoglutarate to alpha-ketoglutarate at about 70 C. The exact downstream redox bookkeeping was not isolated as a single elementary step; this node is therefore a literature-guided, mass-balanced net representation of dehydration plus reduction, with H2 inserted as the geochemically plausible reducing equivalent in warm anoxic Fe2+-rich waters."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Phosphate/glyoxylate conversion to alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006", "mol_012"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["phosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "phosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral pH, concentrated phosphate-rich pool, excess glyoxylate, anoxic."}, "mechanism": "A second glyoxylate acts as reductant while hydroxyketoglutarate undergoes net dehydration-reduction to alpha-ketoglutarate; the sacrificial glyoxylate is oxidized to oxalate.", "reasoning": "Stubbs et al. (2020) reported a metal-free, phosphate-catalyzed alpha-ketoacid network in which glyoxylate acts as both carbon source and reductant, producing alpha-ketoglutarate in about 5% yield at pH 7 and 50 C. Writing oxalate explicitly as the coproduct makes the redox closure transparent and avoids the under-specified shorthand often used for this step."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Fresh-FeS reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["fresh FeS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "323-348K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "fresh FeS", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide-rich hot spring sediment or geothermal precipitate; pH near the NH4+/NH3 pKa; explicit H2 denotes net reducing equivalents."}, "mechanism": "Alpha-ketoglutarate first forms an imine with ammonia, then a reduced FeS surface delivers electrons and protons to give glutamate.", "reasoning": "Freshly precipitated FeS and related reduced iron minerals promote reductive amination of alpha-oxo acids near the pKa of ammonium, and glutamate is among the reported products (Hafenbradl et al., 1995; Huber and Wachtershauser, 2003). Because published glutamate yields are often summarized secondarily and alpha-ketoglutarate is intrinsically the slowest common ketoacid to aminate, this remains a bottleneck rather than a solved step (Mayer and Moran, 2022). The explicit H2 is a net redox bookkeeping device rather than a claim that free H2 was the only laboratory reductant."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Electroreduced-FeS reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["electroreduced FeS"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "electroreduced FeS", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic sulfide interface with natural redox disequilibrium; explicit H2 denotes reducing equivalents stored in the mineral surface."}, "mechanism": "Ammonia adds to the ketoacid and the resulting imine is reduced on an electron-rich FeS surface.", "reasoning": "Kitadai et al. (2019) showed that electrochemically reduced FeS converts alpha-keto acids to amino acids in high yield, with alanine and glutamate favored. The experimental setting was an alkaline-vent analogue; placing the same chemistry at a redox-active surface sulfide interface is an environmental extrapolation, but it retains the strongest direct support for preferential glutamate formation on FeS."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Ni/H2 reductive amination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_007", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310K", "pressure": "Ambient to mildly elevated H2 fugacity", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "pH around 8; NH3 or NH4HCO3-rich pool; competing alpha-hydroxyglutarate formation is expected."}, "mechanism": "Alpha-ketoglutarate forms an imine with ammonia and heterogeneous Ni activates H2 to hydrogenate that intermediate to glutamate.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) demonstrated H2-driven reductive amination of alpha-keto acids over Ni/SiO2-Al2O3, Pd/C, and Ni-bearing meteorite material at 22 C and pH 8 with much lower ammonia than older Fe-based studies. This step is experimentally strong for isolated alpha-keto acids, but their one-pot alpha-ketoglutarate-forming network still did not detect glutamate, and hydroxy acids competed strongly with Ni and meteorite catalysts (Kaur et al., 2024)."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Glycine transamination of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_009"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_001"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-350K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Near-neutral pH, concentrated aqueous pool, anoxic."}, "mechanism": "Amino transfer from glycine to the alpha-ketoacid converts alpha-ketoglutarate to glutamate while oxidizing glycine to glyoxylate.", "reasoning": "Stubbs et al. (2020) reported that alpha-ketoacids from the phosphate/glyoxylate network can be transaminated by glycine, which makes glutamate formation metabolically congruent and avoids mineral-mediated reductive amination. Direct prebiotic alpha-ketoglutarate-to-glutamate data are less complete than for FeS or Ni/H2, so this edge is included as a plausible but weaker closure."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Hydantoin capture of alpha-ketoglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_010", "mol_007"], "outputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["diamidophosphate"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-305K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "diamidophosphate", "aqueous": true, "other": "Cyanide-bearing pool with staged reagent delivery; the node tracks the balanced organic core and does not expand phosphate-containing byproducts from DAP turnover."}, "mechanism": "Cyanide captures the keto carbonyl, ammonia incorporation follows, and DAP-assisted cyclization yields the 5-substituted hydantoin.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) converted alpha-ketoacids, including alpha-ketoglutarate, to hydantoins in very high yield using cyanide with diamidophosphate activation, explicitly proposing this as a bridge between cyanide chemistry and alpha-ketoacid metabolism. The route is experimentally strong for hydantoin formation, but reagent plausibility remains debated because it depends on cyanide and DAP."}, {"id": "rxn_009", "name": "Hydrolysis of glutamate hydantoin", "inputs": ["mol_011", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008", "mol_007", "mol_013"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "350-355K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Warm hydrolysis over prolonged times; incomplete conversion expected."}, "mechanism": "Hydantoin ring opening followed by hydrolytic decarbamylation releases glutamate, ammonia, and carbon dioxide.", "reasoning": "Pulletikurti et al. (2022) demonstrated hydrolytic conversion of the alpha-ketoglutarate hydantoin to glutamate, but the closure was inefficient: the reported preprint data gave about 19% glutamate with most hydantoin still present after hydrolysis. This is therefore a real but low-conversion end step rather than a clean quantitative closure."}, {"id": "rxn_010", "name": "Competitive reduction to alpha-hydroxyglutarate", "inputs": ["mol_006", "mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_015"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["Ni/SiO2-Al2O3", "Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-310K", "pressure": "Ambient to mildly elevated H2 fugacity", "catalyst": "Ni/SiO2-Al2O3 or Ni-bearing meteoritic alloy", "aqueous": true, "other": "Anoxic pH around 8; direct competitor to Ni-mediated amination."}, "mechanism": "Heterogeneous hydrogenation reduces the alpha-keto group directly to the corresponding alpha-hydroxy acid.", "reasoning": "Kaur et al. (2024) observed amino acid and hydroxy acid mixtures from alpha-keto acid hydrogenations, with Ni and meteoritic catalysts often giving near 1:1 amino-to-hydroxy ratios. Including alpha-hydroxyglutarate explicitly captures the main competing sink that erodes glutamate yield; the general kinetic caution is also consistent with Mayer and Moran (2022)."}, {"id": "rxn_011", "name": "Cyclization of glutamate to pyroglutamate", "inputs": ["mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "333-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Accelerated by acidic wet-dry cycles and heating; stronger heating eventually causes further decomposition."}, "mechanism": "Intramolecular condensation of the amino group with the gamma-carboxyl group forms the 5-membered lactam and expels water.", "reasoning": "Glutamate cyclizes to pyroglutamate in warm or acidic aqueous settings and during drying; heating also drives the same dehydration in the solid state (Lee et al., 2014; Wu et al., 2010; Weiss et al., 2018). Because meteoritic work treats pyroglutamate as an acid-labile precursor reservoir for glutamate, this species should be represented as more than a simple dead-end loss channel (Pizzarello and Cooper, 2001)."}, {"id": "rxn_012", "name": "Hydrolytic reopening of pyroglutamate", "inputs": ["mol_014", "mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_008"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": true, "other": "Slow reservoir release under prolonged aqueous exposure or harsher hydrolysis."}, "mechanism": "Lactam hydrolysis reopens the pyroglutamate ring to free glutamate.", "reasoning": "Pyroglutamate can reopen to glutamate under hydrolytic conditions, which is why meteoritic pyroglutamate is treated as an acid-labile glutamate reservoir (Pizzarello and Cooper, 2001). However, reopening is slower and generally harsher than cyclization, so this edge is included as a weak reservoir-release step rather than an efficient equilibrium (Lee et al., 2014)."}], "pathways": [{"pathway_id": "P1", "name": "Fe2+/H2 feeder plus fresh-FeS amination", "description": "Hydroxyketoglutarate is formed by aldol chemistry, converted to alpha-ketoglutarate by the Fe2+/H2 net route, and then reductively aminated on fresh FeS.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P2", "name": "Fe2+/H2 feeder plus electroreduced-FeS amination", "description": "The Fe2+/H2 feeder generates alpha-ketoglutarate, which is then aminated on an electron-rich FeS surface.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P3", "name": "Fe2+/H2 feeder plus Ni/H2 amination", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate from the Fe2+/H2 feeder is hydrogenatively aminated on Ni-bearing mineral or meteoritic surfaces.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P4", "name": "Fe2+/H2 feeder plus glycine transamination", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate from the Fe2+/H2 feeder accepts nitrogen from glycine, regenerating glyoxylate and producing glutamate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P5", "name": "Fe2+/H2 feeder plus hydantoin bridge", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate from the Fe2+/H2 feeder is trapped as a glutamate hydantoin and then hydrolyzed to glutamate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_002", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_011"]}, {"pathway_id": "P6", "name": "Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus fresh-FeS amination", "description": "The phosphate/glyoxylate route generates alpha-ketoglutarate with oxalate coproduct, then fresh FeS closes the network to glutamate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_004"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P7", "name": "Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus electroreduced-FeS amination", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate made by the phosphate/glyoxylate route is aminated on an electron-rich FeS surface.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_005"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P8", "name": "Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus Ni/H2 amination", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate formed in the phosphate-driven alpha-ketoacid network is converted to glutamate by Ni-catalyzed hydrogenative amination.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_006"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P9", "name": "Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus glycine transamination", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate from the phosphate/glyoxylate feeder is transaminated by glycine to produce glutamate and recycle glyoxylate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_007"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006"]}, {"pathway_id": "P10", "name": "Phosphate/glyoxylate feeder plus hydantoin bridge", "description": "Alpha-ketoglutarate from the phosphate/glyoxylate feeder is captured by cyanide as a hydantoin and hydrolyzed to glutamate.", "reaction_sequence": ["rxn_001", "rxn_003", "rxn_008", "rxn_009"], "key_intermediates": ["mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_011"]}], "hub_molecules": ["mol_001", "mol_003", "mol_006", "mol_008"], "convergence_points": [{"molecule_id": "mol_003", "incoming_reactions": ["rxn_001"], "outgo